From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 13:19:53 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 25/48] net: xpon: omci: describe ONU data, ONU-G, ONU2-G,
 ANI-G and OLT-G

These ONU-created MEs had no attribute descriptor, so the agent kept
them as untyped shadows (ONU-G and ONU2-G only had a private layout for
Get). A Set copied the attribute values to offset 0 of the stored
instance whatever attributes it named, and a Get returned the stored
bytes from offset 0 under the requested mask. G.988 Set and Get work per
attribute, in attribute order, with the sizes of the ME definition.

In practice a Set of an ONU-G attribute such as the battery backup or
the administrative state wiped the vendor ID, version and serial number
that the following Gets returned, until the next MIB reset; a Set of the
ONU2-G security mode did the same to the equipment ID and OMCC version;
a Set of an ANI-G threshold landed on the SR indication.

Give the five MEs descriptors with their G.988 attributes (9.1.1,
9.1.2, 9.1.3, 9.2.1, 9.12.2), keeping the stored offsets the identity
code already uses. ANI-G optical levels asked for together with other
attributes are now measured as well, and fail when the optical frontend
cannot provide them.

What a Get returns does not change for any mask the agent answered
before, checked by comparing every attribute mask in userspace, with two
deliberate exceptions:
  * the ONU-G extended TC-layer options attribute is 2 bytes in G.988,
    not 1;
  * the ONU2-G connectivity capability and priority queue scale factor
    keep reading as 0, as they always have; the values that were stored
    at offsets no Get read are dropped instead of being exposed now.
The ANI-G SR indication and the other ANI-G defaults stay as they are.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
